1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,637
Hi, I'm Ryan Rover.

2
00:00:01,817 --> 00:00:04,449
Every week there's a new headline about

3
00:00:04,449 --> 00:00:05,462
AI taking jobs.

4
00:00:05,642 --> 00:00:06,909
It writes emails,

5
00:00:06,909 --> 00:00:10,563
designs logos, answers customer service calls and

6
00:00:10,563 --> 00:00:11,830
even writes code.

7
00:00:12,010 --> 00:00:14,601
If you're thinking about starting or buying

8
00:00:14,601 --> 00:00:16,770
a business, that's a scary backdrop.

9
00:00:16,950 --> 00:00:19,727
Why build something a machine might replace

10
00:00:19,727 --> 00:00:20,631
in five years?

11
00:00:20,811 --> 00:00:23,873
So today, we're going through fifteen businesses

12
00:00:23,873 --> 00:00:25,276
that AI can't replace,

13
00:00:25,276 --> 00:00:26,680
why they're protected,

14
00:00:26,680 --> 00:00:27,956
how they make money,

15
00:00:27,956 --> 00:00:29,934
and the twist most people miss:

16
00:00:29,934 --> 00:00:32,549
how AI can actually make these businesses

17
00:00:32,549 --> 00:00:33,570
more profitable.

18
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:36,694
Fifteen businesses AI can't replace.

19
00:00:36,874 --> 00:00:37,910
Built on hands,

20
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:40,601
presence, trust and the physical world.

21
00:00:40,952 --> 00:00:42,907
Let's start with what AI is good

22
00:00:42,907 --> 00:00:44,191
at and what it isn't.

23
00:00:44,371 --> 00:00:47,030
AI is very good at working with

24
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:49,432
information: words, numbers,

25
00:00:49,432 --> 00:00:51,662
images, code and patterns.

26
00:00:51,842 --> 00:00:54,544
It struggles with the messy physical world:

27
00:00:54,544 --> 00:00:55,989
crawling under a house,

28
00:00:55,989 --> 00:00:57,434
calming a scared child,

29
00:00:57,434 --> 00:00:59,696
fixing a leaking pipe behind a wall,

30
00:00:59,696 --> 00:01:01,770
or reading the mood of a grieving

31
00:01:01,770 --> 00:01:02,209
family.

32
00:01:02,389 --> 00:01:03,826
Robots are improving,

33
00:01:03,826 --> 00:01:06,974
but robots that can handle unpredictable homes

34
00:01:06,974 --> 00:01:09,369
and people at a reasonable cost are

35
00:01:09,369 --> 00:01:11,422
still far away for most tasks.

36
00:01:11,772 --> 00:01:13,564
So our test for this list is

37
00:01:13,564 --> 00:01:14,012
simple.

38
00:01:14,192 --> 00:01:16,119
A business makes it if the core

39
00:01:16,119 --> 00:01:19,103
work needs human hands in unpredictable physical

40
00:01:19,103 --> 00:01:22,336
spaces, human presence that customers actually want,

41
00:01:22,336 --> 00:01:24,760
or human trust and judgment that people

42
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:26,998
aren't willing to hand to a machine.

43
00:01:27,178 --> 00:01:29,263
The more of those three a business

44
00:01:29,263 --> 00:01:31,102
has, the more protected it is.

45
00:01:31,453 --> 00:01:32,346
A quick warning.

46
00:01:32,526 --> 00:01:35,499
Nobody knows exactly how fast technology will

47
00:01:35,499 --> 00:01:38,141
change, and some parts of every business

48
00:01:38,141 --> 00:01:39,330
will be automated.

49
00:01:39,510 --> 00:01:42,214
Any income numbers here are illustrations,

50
00:01:42,214 --> 00:01:43,051
not promises.

51
00:01:43,231 --> 00:01:44,955
The goal is to help you think

52
00:01:44,955 --> 00:01:47,987
clearly about which businesses have durable demand,

53
00:01:47,987 --> 00:01:49,652
so you can build or buy with

54
00:01:49,652 --> 00:01:50,603
more confidence.

55
00:01:50,954 --> 00:01:52,715
We'll go through all fifteen,

56
00:01:52,715 --> 00:01:54,901
then look at them from three angles.

57
00:01:55,081 --> 00:01:57,669
The numbers, meaning how AI can lower

58
00:01:57,669 --> 00:02:00,747
costs and raise profits in these businesses.

59
00:02:00,927 --> 00:02:03,368
The psychology, meaning why fear of AI

60
00:02:03,368 --> 00:02:04,653
makes people freeze,

61
00:02:04,653 --> 00:02:06,581
and why that's an opportunity.

62
00:02:06,761 --> 00:02:07,764
And the playbook,

63
00:02:07,764 --> 00:02:09,711
meaning how to choose one and use

64
00:02:09,711 --> 00:02:11,835
AI as your assistant instead of your

65
00:02:11,835 --> 00:02:12,484
competitor.

66
00:02:12,835 --> 00:02:14,379
Let's start with the trades.

67
00:02:14,729 --> 00:02:17,738
Employment projections come from the Bureau of

68
00:02:17,738 --> 00:02:20,812
Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook.

69
00:02:20,992 --> 00:02:23,372
Information about aging comes from the U.

70
00:02:23,552 --> 00:02:23,889
S.

71
00:02:24,069 --> 00:02:25,149
Census Bureau.

72
00:02:25,329 --> 00:02:27,581
Small business data comes from the U.

73
00:02:27,761 --> 00:02:28,132
S.

74
00:02:28,312 --> 00:02:30,472
Small Business Administration.

75
00:02:30,652 --> 00:02:33,903
Example numbers are simplified illustrations.

76
00:02:34,083 --> 00:02:35,872
This video is education,

77
00:02:35,872 --> 00:02:38,854
not financial, legal or business advice.

78
00:02:39,205 --> 00:02:40,052
Number 1.

79
00:02:40,232 --> 00:02:42,647
Electrical and plumbing contracting.

80
00:02:42,827 --> 00:02:44,704
every building is different,

81
00:02:44,704 --> 00:02:46,113
every job is a puzzle

82
00:02:46,463 --> 00:02:48,123
Why AI can't replace it.

83
00:02:48,303 --> 00:02:49,696
Every house is different.

84
00:02:49,876 --> 00:02:52,100
The wiring in a 1960s home looks

85
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:53,837
nothing like a new build,

86
00:02:53,837 --> 00:02:56,269
and the pipe behind a bathroom wall

87
00:02:56,269 --> 00:02:57,381
could be copper,

88
00:02:57,381 --> 00:02:59,257
plastic or something older.

89
00:02:59,437 --> 00:03:02,617
A licensed electrician or plumber walks in,

90
00:03:02,617 --> 00:03:05,205
diagnoses an unpredictable problem,

91
00:03:05,205 --> 00:03:07,423
and fixes it with their hands,

92
00:03:07,423 --> 00:03:08,533
often in tight,

93
00:03:08,533 --> 00:03:09,642
awkward spaces.

94
00:03:09,822 --> 00:03:11,824
Robots that can do that across millions

95
00:03:11,824 --> 00:03:13,723
of different homes are nowhere close.

96
00:03:14,073 --> 00:03:15,048
How it makes money.

97
00:03:15,228 --> 00:03:17,060
Service calls, repairs,

98
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:19,767
installations and larger projects.

99
00:03:19,947 --> 00:03:23,573
Many contractors build recurring revenue with maintenance

100
00:03:23,573 --> 00:03:26,689
plans and partnerships with builders and property

101
00:03:26,689 --> 00:03:27,262
managers.

102
00:03:27,442 --> 00:03:28,920
Licensing takes years,

103
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:30,600
which limits competition.

104
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:33,999
And with many experienced tradespeople retiring,

105
00:03:33,999 --> 00:03:37,018
skilled contractors may see strong demand for

106
00:03:37,018 --> 00:03:37,420
years.

107
00:03:37,771 --> 00:03:39,361
How AI actually helps it.

108
00:03:39,541 --> 00:03:41,986
AI can answer the phone after hours,

109
00:03:41,986 --> 00:03:44,838
book appointments, write estimates faster,

110
00:03:44,838 --> 00:03:47,214
help diagnose problems from photos,

111
00:03:47,214 --> 00:03:49,862
and remind customers about maintenance.

112
00:03:50,042 --> 00:03:52,523
That means fewer missed calls and less

113
00:03:52,523 --> 00:03:55,135
office work, so the contractor can spend

114
00:03:55,135 --> 00:03:56,637
more time on paid jobs.

115
00:03:56,987 --> 00:03:58,113
Getting started.

116
00:03:58,293 --> 00:04:01,118
Most electricians and plumbers start with a

117
00:04:01,118 --> 00:04:04,074
paid apprenticeship that lasts several years,

118
00:04:04,074 --> 00:04:07,030
then earn a journeyman license and eventually

119
00:04:07,030 --> 00:04:09,067
a master or contractor license.

120
00:04:09,247 --> 00:04:11,351
Many work for others for years,

121
00:04:11,351 --> 00:04:13,590
learning both the trade and how a

122
00:04:13,590 --> 00:04:15,626
company prices and sells jobs,

123
00:04:15,626 --> 00:04:18,272
before starting their own or buying one

124
00:04:18,272 --> 00:04:19,765
from a retiring owner.

125
00:04:20,116 --> 00:04:20,963
Number 2.

126
00:04:21,143 --> 00:04:22,269
Home remodeling.

127
00:04:22,449 --> 00:04:25,944
kitchens, bathrooms and basements built by hand

128
00:04:26,294 --> 00:04:27,977
Why AI can't replace it.

129
00:04:28,157 --> 00:04:31,149
Remodeling means tearing out old materials,

130
00:04:31,149 --> 00:04:33,584
discovering surprises behind walls,

131
00:04:33,584 --> 00:04:36,575
adjusting plans on the fly and coordinating

132
00:04:36,575 --> 00:04:37,410
many trades.

133
00:04:37,590 --> 00:04:39,657
It also means sitting at a kitchen

134
00:04:39,657 --> 00:04:41,967
table with a nervous couple spending a

135
00:04:41,967 --> 00:04:43,608
big chunk of their savings,

136
00:04:43,608 --> 00:04:45,067
and earning their trust.

137
00:04:45,247 --> 00:04:47,438
That mix of physical work and human

138
00:04:47,438 --> 00:04:49,879
judgment is extremely hard to automate.

139
00:04:50,230 --> 00:04:51,228
How it makes money.

140
00:04:51,408 --> 00:04:54,305
Remodelers earn money on large projects like

141
00:04:54,305 --> 00:04:57,004
kitchens, bathrooms, decks and additions.

142
00:04:57,184 --> 00:04:59,979
Margins depend on accurate estimates,

143
00:04:59,979 --> 00:05:03,906
good project management and reliable subcontractors.

144
00:05:04,086 --> 00:05:06,798
A company with a strong reputation can

145
00:05:06,798 --> 00:05:09,438
keep a long waiting list of projects.

146
00:05:09,789 --> 00:05:11,286
How AI actually helps it.

147
00:05:11,466 --> 00:05:14,508
AI can create design renderings so customers

148
00:05:14,508 --> 00:05:17,066
can see their new kitchen before work

149
00:05:17,066 --> 00:05:19,209
begins, produce material lists,

150
00:05:19,209 --> 00:05:22,113
draft schedules and send progress updates.

151
00:05:22,293 --> 00:05:24,144
Customers feel more informed,

152
00:05:24,144 --> 00:05:26,507
and the remodeler spends less time on

153
00:05:26,507 --> 00:05:27,146
paperwork.

154
00:05:27,496 --> 00:05:28,494
Getting started.

155
00:05:28,674 --> 00:05:30,945
Many remodelers come from a trade or

156
00:05:30,945 --> 00:05:32,459
from project management.

157
00:05:32,639 --> 00:05:35,925
Start with smaller jobs like bathrooms and

158
00:05:35,925 --> 00:05:38,898
decks, build a portfolio of before and

159
00:05:38,898 --> 00:05:42,809
after photos, and line up reliable subcontractors.

160
00:05:42,989 --> 00:05:46,192
Licensing requirements vary by state and job

161
00:05:46,192 --> 00:05:48,376
size, so check before you bid.

162
00:05:48,727 --> 00:05:49,597
Number 3.

163
00:05:49,777 --> 00:05:51,960
Auto body and collision repair.

164
00:05:52,140 --> 00:05:53,754
every crash is unique

165
00:05:54,104 --> 00:05:55,868
Why AI can't replace it.

166
00:05:56,048 --> 00:05:57,848
No two accidents are the same.

167
00:05:58,028 --> 00:06:00,749
A collision shop has to assess damage,

168
00:06:00,749 --> 00:06:04,115
straighten frames, repair or replace panels and

169
00:06:04,115 --> 00:06:05,690
match paint perfectly.

170
00:06:05,870 --> 00:06:07,854
Cars are getting more complex,

171
00:06:07,854 --> 00:06:10,631
with sensors and cameras that need careful

172
00:06:10,631 --> 00:06:12,349
calibration after repairs,

173
00:06:12,349 --> 00:06:14,465
which raises the skill required,

174
00:06:14,465 --> 00:06:15,391
not lowers it.

175
00:06:15,741 --> 00:06:16,739
How it makes money.

176
00:06:16,919 --> 00:06:19,370
Collision shops earn much of their revenue

177
00:06:19,370 --> 00:06:20,828
through insurance claims,

178
00:06:20,828 --> 00:06:23,045
which creates steady demand as long as

179
00:06:23,045 --> 00:06:23,804
people drive.

180
00:06:23,984 --> 00:06:27,193
Shops that build relationships with insurers and

181
00:06:27,193 --> 00:06:30,804
earn certifications from car manufacturers can attract

182
00:06:30,804 --> 00:06:31,472
more work.

183
00:06:31,823 --> 00:06:33,378
How AI actually helps it.

184
00:06:33,558 --> 00:06:36,536
AI tools can estimate damage from photos,

185
00:06:36,536 --> 00:06:39,804
speed up insurance paperwork and order parts.

186
00:06:39,984 --> 00:06:41,785
That shortens repair times,

187
00:06:41,785 --> 00:06:44,519
which keeps customers and insurers happy,

188
00:06:44,519 --> 00:06:46,786
and lets the shop handle more cars

189
00:06:46,786 --> 00:06:48,053
with the same team.

190
00:06:48,404 --> 00:06:49,390
Getting started.

191
00:06:49,570 --> 00:06:53,977
Technicians train through vocational programs and certifications,

192
00:06:53,977 --> 00:06:56,350
and many owners worked in shops for

193
00:06:56,350 --> 00:06:56,757
years.

194
00:06:56,937 --> 00:07:00,260
Buying an existing shop with insurer relationships

195
00:07:00,260 --> 00:07:02,254
is a common path to ownership,

196
00:07:02,254 --> 00:07:05,245
because those relationships are hard to build

197
00:07:05,245 --> 00:07:06,109
from scratch.

198
00:07:06,459 --> 00:07:07,353
Number 4.

199
00:07:07,533 --> 00:07:09,413
Home health and senior care.

200
00:07:09,593 --> 00:07:11,468
older adults want people,

201
00:07:11,468 --> 00:07:12,368
not machines

202
00:07:12,718 --> 00:07:14,447
Why AI can't replace it.

203
00:07:14,627 --> 00:07:16,634
Helping an older person bathe,

204
00:07:16,634 --> 00:07:19,644
dress, take medication and move safely around

205
00:07:19,644 --> 00:07:21,852
their home requires gentle hands,

206
00:07:21,852 --> 00:07:23,323
patience and judgment.

207
00:07:23,503 --> 00:07:26,026
And companionship is a big part of

208
00:07:26,026 --> 00:07:26,917
the service.

209
00:07:27,097 --> 00:07:29,264
Most families don't want a robot caring

210
00:07:29,264 --> 00:07:30,208
for their mother.

211
00:07:30,388 --> 00:07:31,564
They want a kind,

212
00:07:31,564 --> 00:07:35,022
trustworthy person, and the Census Bureau projects

213
00:07:35,022 --> 00:07:37,927
the older population will keep growing for

214
00:07:37,927 --> 00:07:38,480
decades.

215
00:07:38,831 --> 00:07:39,852
How it makes money.

216
00:07:40,032 --> 00:07:42,354
Home care agencies charge by the hour

217
00:07:42,354 --> 00:07:44,990
for caregivers who help with daily living.

218
00:07:45,170 --> 00:07:47,398
Some services are paid privately,

219
00:07:47,398 --> 00:07:49,897
others by long term care insurance or

220
00:07:49,897 --> 00:07:52,936
government programs, depending on the type of

221
00:07:52,936 --> 00:07:53,274
care.

222
00:07:53,454 --> 00:07:55,277
Demand is strong in many areas,

223
00:07:55,277 --> 00:07:57,747
and the biggest challenge is often finding

224
00:07:57,747 --> 00:07:58,806
enough caregivers.

225
00:07:59,156 --> 00:08:00,653
How AI actually helps it.

226
00:08:00,833 --> 00:08:03,605
AI can help schedule caregivers efficiently,

227
00:08:03,605 --> 00:08:06,440
track visits, handle billing and flag changes

228
00:08:06,440 --> 00:08:08,771
in a client's routine that might need

229
00:08:08,771 --> 00:08:09,401
attention.

230
00:08:09,581 --> 00:08:11,944
That frees managers to focus on recruiting

231
00:08:11,944 --> 00:08:13,407
and supporting caregivers,

232
00:08:13,407 --> 00:08:15,038
which is the real bottleneck.

233
00:08:15,388 --> 00:08:16,409
Getting started.

234
00:08:16,589 --> 00:08:19,398
Non medical home care agencies can start

235
00:08:19,398 --> 00:08:21,856
with modest capital in many states,

236
00:08:21,856 --> 00:08:23,822
though licensing rules vary.

237
00:08:24,002 --> 00:08:26,816
Some owners choose a franchise for training

238
00:08:26,816 --> 00:08:27,601
and systems.

239
00:08:27,781 --> 00:08:30,246
The key skill isn't caregiving itself,

240
00:08:30,246 --> 00:08:33,749
but recruiting, training and keeping great caregivers.

241
00:08:34,099 --> 00:08:34,958
Number 5.

242
00:08:35,138 --> 00:08:37,193
Physical therapy and massage.

243
00:08:37,373 --> 00:08:39,045
healing through human hands

244
00:08:39,395 --> 00:08:41,183
Why AI can't replace it.

245
00:08:41,363 --> 00:08:44,710
Physical therapists and massage therapists work with

246
00:08:44,710 --> 00:08:46,963
their hands, feeling tight muscles,

247
00:08:46,963 --> 00:08:49,602
adjusting movements and reacting to how a

248
00:08:49,602 --> 00:08:51,533
patient responds in real time.

249
00:08:51,713 --> 00:08:54,886
Patients recovering from surgery or injury also

250
00:08:54,886 --> 00:08:58,195
need encouragement and accountability from a real

251
00:08:58,195 --> 00:08:58,667
person.

252
00:08:59,018 --> 00:09:00,179
How it makes money.

253
00:09:00,359 --> 00:09:03,761
Physical therapy clinics bill insurance or patients

254
00:09:03,761 --> 00:09:07,564
directly, while massage businesses often sell memberships

255
00:09:07,564 --> 00:09:09,031
with monthly sessions.

256
00:09:09,211 --> 00:09:12,903
Memberships create recurring revenue and loyal clients.

257
00:09:13,083 --> 00:09:14,463
Both require licenses,

258
00:09:14,463 --> 00:09:16,659
which protects those who have them.

259
00:09:17,009 --> 00:09:18,564
How AI actually helps it.

260
00:09:18,744 --> 00:09:21,334
AI can create home exercise programs,

261
00:09:21,334 --> 00:09:22,943
track patient progress,

262
00:09:22,943 --> 00:09:26,372
send reminders and handle scheduling and billing.

263
00:09:26,552 --> 00:09:29,366
Patients stay on track between visits,

264
00:09:29,366 --> 00:09:32,624
and therapists spend less time on paperwork.

265
00:09:32,974 --> 00:09:34,007
Getting started.

266
00:09:34,187 --> 00:09:37,195
Physical therapists need a doctoral degree and

267
00:09:37,195 --> 00:09:40,596
a license, while massage therapists usually complete

268
00:09:40,596 --> 00:09:43,278
a shorter training program and licensing.

269
00:09:43,458 --> 00:09:46,363
Many massage therapists start by renting a

270
00:09:46,363 --> 00:09:48,368
room, building a client list,

271
00:09:48,368 --> 00:09:51,619
then opening their own studio with memberships.

272
00:09:51,970 --> 00:09:53,015
Number 6.

273
00:09:53,195 --> 00:09:55,923
Personal training and fitness studios.

274
00:09:56,103 --> 00:09:58,019
people pay for accountability

275
00:09:58,369 --> 00:10:00,099
Why AI can't replace it.

276
00:10:00,279 --> 00:10:02,971
There are countless free workout videos and

277
00:10:02,971 --> 00:10:03,785
fitness apps.

278
00:10:03,965 --> 00:10:06,403
Yet people still pay personal trainers.

279
00:10:06,583 --> 00:10:06,815
Why?

280
00:10:06,995 --> 00:10:09,178
Because what they're buying isn't information.

281
00:10:09,358 --> 00:10:11,399
It's accountability, motivation,

282
00:10:11,399 --> 00:10:13,759
correct form and a person who notices

283
00:10:13,759 --> 00:10:15,290
when they're struggling.

284
00:10:15,470 --> 00:10:18,389
Group fitness studios also sell community,

285
00:10:18,389 --> 00:10:20,544
which no app can fully replace.

286
00:10:20,894 --> 00:10:21,915
